AU  - Pan, S.
AU  - Gu, T.
AU  - Zeng, J.
PY  - 2023
TI  - Identifying the driving forces of global ecosystem services balance, 2000-2020
JO  - Journal of Cleaner Production
VL  - 426
SP  - 139019
DO  - 10.1016/j.jclepro.2023.139019
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jclepro.2023.139019
N1  - REAL, CLOSE COMPETITOR — the closest one found. Full citation confirmed by the user directly
  (2026-08-25) — the earlier search-based author lookup was blocked by bot protection on the
  publisher site, not because the info didn't exist. Full text obtained and read 2026-08-26 —
  corrects two errors from the earlier search-snippet-only characterization: this is COUNTY-level
  analysis, not grid-resolution (the authors themselves flag grid-scale as needed future work —
  "we can experiment with grid-scale in ESs balance measurement in the future," §4.3), and it does
  NOT use InVEST at all (no mention anywhere in the paper) — supply is computed via a
  Costanza-tradition equivalent-factor-table value index (VC_ij × land-use area, NPP-corrected,
  Eq. 1), not process-based biophysical modeling. Also not "four services" — supply is a SINGLE
  aggregate composite value index (ESVI) spanning Costanza's four regulating/two provisioning/two
  supporting/one cultural service categories, not per-service physical metrics. Demand side:
  LUI × ln(population density) × ln(GDP density) (Eq. 3); balance = standardized supply minus
  standardized demand (Eq. 4). Three continents/time-point analysis (2000/2010/2020), driving
  forces via Geodetector (spatial-variance decomposition, not regression in the traditional sense
  — paired with OLS for sign). Authors also self-acknowledge (§4.3) that their demand measure
  conflates potential and actual demand — a real, stated uncertainty in their own design. No
  hotspot definition, no beneficiary/exposure analysis, no land-cover-conversion attribution test.
  Should be cited and distinguished directly — this is the paper most likely to come up if a
  reviewer or reader searches for prior work, so silently not citing it is riskier than citing and
  distinguishing it. Their own self-identified limitations (grid-scale, demand conflation) are
  stronger material for the Discussion than an asserted methodological advantage would be.
ER  -
